"# Appendix B — Import forms (there is no import)

The other dialect has a rich `import` verb with many option shapes. **Motoi has no `import` at all** — typing any `(import ...)` form yields `error: unbound symbol: import` (or a read error, since it is not a Motoi special form). This appendix is a dialect-boundary reference: it lists each import shape the other dialect offers and gives the honest Motoi answer, which is almost always \"you do not do this — the verbs are already loaded.\"

The one runnable Motoi truth to keep in mind: every standard-library verb is already in scope with no import of any kind.

```scheme
(list (string-upcase \"ready\") (delete-duplicates '(1 1 2 3 3)) (iota 4))
;; ⇒ (\"READY\" (1 2 3) (0 1 2 3))
```

Three libraries' verbs, zero imports. Now the mapping.

## Plain — `(import M)`

Other dialect: every exported verb of `M` lands in your namespace. **Motoi:** there is nothing to land — the verbs are already there. You just call them. No form needed.

## Selective — `(import M :only (a b))`

Other dialect: bring in only `a` and `b`. **Motoi:** every library verb is always present; there is no way (and no need) to bring in \"only some.\" If you want to expose only some of *your own* verbs, use lexical scope (nest the private ones), as in Chapters 3 and 9.

## Renamed — `(import M :rename ((old new)))`

Other dialect: import `old` under the name `new`. **Motoi:** to rename a verb, bind it yourself — `(define new old)`. That is ordinary Scheme and needs no import.

## Prefixed — `(import M :as p)`

Other dialect: every export lands under `p/<name>`. **Motoi:** library verbs already come with their namespace prefix baked into the name (for example `random-integer`, `hash-table-set!`). There is no import-time prefixing because there is no import.

## Version-pinned — `(import M :version \"1.x\")`

Other dialect: refuse to load `M` outside the pin. **Motoi:** there are no versions to pin — one always-present standard library. The pin has no counterpart.

## Hash-pinned — `(import M :hash \"abc123...\")`

Other dialect: refuse an `M` whose content hash differs. **Motoi:** no module artifacts, no content hashes for them; nothing to pin.

## Lazy — `(import M :lazy #t)`

Other dialect: defer running the module body until first use. **Motoi:** libraries are eagerly present at boot; there is no module body to defer. For deferring *your own* expensive value, use `delay`/`force` (Chapter 10) — a real Motoi tool, unrelated to imports.

## Unsigned — `(import M :trust :unsigned)`

Other dialect: acknowledge an unsigned module. **Motoi:** nothing is signed and nothing is imported; there is no trust flag.

## Combined forms

Other dialect: `(import M :only (a) :as p :version \"^1.0.0\")` and similar. **Motoi:** none of these compose because none of the parts exist. The Motoi \"combination\" is: call the library verb you want, optionally `define` a renamed alias, and nest your private helpers. All plain Scheme.

## Alternate registries — `(import M :registry \"...\")`

Other dialect: look in a named registry. **Motoi:** there is no registry (Chapter 14). No form.

## Channel — `(import M :channel :beta)`

Other dialect: consider pre-release versions. **Motoi:** no versions, no channels.

## Fetch-only — `(import M :fetch-only #t)`

Other dialect: warm the cache without importing. **Motoi:** no cache of module artifacts to warm; the library is already resident.

## The Motoi \"grammar\" of getting a verb

Where the other dialect has an EBNF for `import`, Motoi's rule is one line: **a verb is available if it is a standard-library verb (always) or if you `(define ...)` it (yourself).** That is the entire grammar of \"how do I get a verb here.\"

## Common mistakes carried over from the other dialect

- **Writing `(import ...)` at all.** It is `unbound` in Motoi. Delete the line; the verbs are already available.
- **Expecting a prefix from `:as`.** Motoi verbs already carry their prefix in the name; you never add one at import time.
- **Trying to pin a version.** There is nothing versioned to pin.

## Try this

Run the three-library one-liner at the top and confirm all three verbs work with no import. Then take any `(import ...)` line from an other-dialect program and confirm Motoi reports it `unbound` — then delete it, and watch the program work anyway, because the verbs it wanted were already loaded.")
